<header><p class="enc-kicker">Event</p><h1 class="enc-title">Delhi Durbar</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1877, 1903, 1911</span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/delhi">Delhi</a> Durbars were imperial assemblies held at Delhi to mark the accession of British monarchs as rulers of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>. The first, in 1877, proclaimed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/queen-victoria">Queen Victoria</a> Empress of India; the second, in 1903, celebrated Edward VII; and the third, in 1911, was attended in person by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/george-v">George V</a> and Queen Mary, who announced the reversal of the partition of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bengal">Bengal</a> and the move of the capital from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kolkata">Calcutta</a> to Delhi.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/delhi-durbar.jpg" alt="Delhi Durbar" width="320" height="178" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown photographer · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Delhi_Durbar,_1911.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
